{"author":{"id":"f4e4dbe251fbd7519eab696986bae317e93a786739d6cebe9c4c1e89dd44e465"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793681968,"nonce":"ETdnvM8cCD4oGdAcIcmbeAnb0jU=","title":"Record $eq compares a closed-class field by instance, while the CLR calls its Equals","message":"Found by the M4 code review (correctness reviewer). Listed under \"Not addressed here\" in https://github.com/icosahedron/csharp-ansatz/pull/1. Parent epic: `54c6e06f383039f9758f1571999c683db1c244561561998cf9a52c41b84fa52f`.\n\nA record's generated `$eq` compares a field whose type is a `[Closed]` class by `$index`, which is instance identity. The CLR's generated record `Equals` calls `EqualityComparer\u003cT\u003e.Default.Equals` on that field. That call reaches the class's own `Equals` if it has one.\n\nThe two agree only under assumption KTD11 (plan `docs/plans/2026-11-02-1245-feat-m4-pure-subset-plan.md`): a closed class's equality is true exactly for identical instances. Two things guard it today:\n\n- extraction records the assumption in the assumption report;\n- `tests/Warrant.Engine.Tests/Differential/ClosedEqualityTests.cs` checks every pair of instances, but only for the corpus's types.\n\nFor a user's closed class with its own `Equals` that is not identity, the model's record equality would differ from the CLR's, with only an assumption in the report to say so.\n\nOptions:\n\n- Check KTD11 at verification time for the user's own classes, for example by running the same pairwise check against the compiled assembly when M5a's build task has it.\n- Reject a record field whose closed-class type has its own equality, until that check exists.\n\nAlso add tests for record `$eq` over a closed-class field. See the related test-gaps ticket.","files":null}]}